1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,061
Lesson 1.5.

2
00:00:02,441 --> 00:00:03,934
Landauer's principle.

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:07,144
Your phone heats up when it works.

4
00:00:07,524 --> 00:00:12,790
Every operation a processor performs
ends up, sooner or later, as heat.

5
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:16,514
The question of this lesson
is surprisingly deep:

6
00:00:16,894 --> 00:00:21,414
is there a minimum cost, imposed by
physics, for processing information?

7
00:00:21,794 --> 00:00:26,101
In 1961 Rolf Landauer
gave a precise answer.

8
00:00:26,481 --> 00:00:30,531
Not every operation costs
something, but one does: erasing.

9
00:00:30,911 --> 00:00:33,777
Erasing a bit has a
minimum price in energy,

10
00:00:33,777 --> 00:00:36,577
and that price depends
only on temperature.

11
00:00:37,683 --> 00:00:40,903
Picture the simplest
possible memory: a box with

12
00:00:40,903 --> 00:00:43,787
a single particle and
a wall in the middle.

13
00:00:44,167 --> 00:00:46,816
If the particle is on the left, the bit is

14
00:00:46,816 --> 00:00:49,275
zero; if it is on the right, it is one.

15
00:00:49,655 --> 00:00:53,559
Erasing means leaving the bit at zero
regardless of its previous value.

16
00:00:53,939 --> 00:00:56,484
One way to do it is to remove the wall and

17
00:00:56,484 --> 00:00:59,088
push a piston from the
right to the middle.

18
00:00:59,468 --> 00:01:02,280
At the end, the particle
is always on the left.

19
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:06,481
Notice what happened: the
available space was cut in half.

20
00:01:06,861 --> 00:01:10,376
Compressing something in contact
with a heat bath requires

21
00:01:10,376 --> 00:01:13,769
work, and that work leaves
as heat into the environment.

22
00:01:14,883 --> 00:01:18,639
Now a more realistic version:
a double-well potential.

23
00:01:19,019 --> 00:01:23,144
Each amber dot is a copy of the
memory, a particle jittering

24
00:01:23,144 --> 00:01:26,788
from thermal noise, simulated
with Langevin dynamics.

25
00:01:27,168 --> 00:01:30,926
At the start, half the
copies are in the left well

26
00:01:30,926 --> 00:01:34,082
and half in the right: the bit is unknown.

27
00:01:34,462 --> 00:01:36,868
The erasure protocol has three steps.

28
00:01:37,248 --> 00:01:41,061
First we lower the barrier,
and the particles mix freely.

29
00:01:41,441 --> 00:01:46,914
Then we tilt the potential to the left,
and they all slide into the zero well.

30
00:01:47,294 --> 00:01:49,410
Finally we raise the barrier again.

31
00:01:49,790 --> 00:01:53,057
The green histogram shows
the distribution: it started

32
00:01:53,057 --> 00:01:56,021
with two equal peaks and
ended with a single one.

33
00:01:56,401 --> 00:01:58,238
We lost one bit of uncertainty.

34
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:01,035
Let's put numbers on that loss.

35
00:02:01,415 --> 00:02:07,673
The entropy of the memory
is S = −k_B Σ p ln p.

36
00:02:08,053 --> 00:02:15,255
Before erasure, each state has probability
one half, so the entropy is k_B ln 2.

37
00:02:15,635 --> 00:02:20,118
Afterwards, state zero has probability
one, and the entropy is zero.

38
00:02:20,498 --> 00:02:24,090
The memory lost k_B ln 2 of entropy.

39
00:02:24,470 --> 00:02:26,627
But the second law requires that the total

40
00:02:26,627 --> 00:02:28,783
entropy of the universe does not decrease.

41
00:02:29,163 --> 00:02:32,740
Therefore the environment must
gain at least that amount.

42
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:38,030
An environment at temperature T that
receives heat Q gains entropy Q/T.

43
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:44,534
Solving, the dissipated heat
satisfies Q ≥ k_B T ln 2.

44
00:02:44,914 --> 00:02:46,613
This is the Landauer bound.

45
00:02:47,717 --> 00:02:53,050
We can reach the same result
with the free energy, F = U − TS.

46
00:02:53,430 --> 00:02:56,309
In an isothermal process,
the work we do on the

47
00:02:56,309 --> 00:02:59,066
system is at least the
change in free energy.

48
00:02:59,446 --> 00:03:04,850
Since the two wells are symmetric, the
internal energy does not change: ΔU = 0.

49
00:03:05,230 --> 00:03:09,193
So the change in free
energy is minus T times the

50
00:03:09,193 --> 00:03:12,672
change in entropy,
which equals k_B T ln 2.

51
00:03:13,052 --> 00:03:15,907
And since the internal
energy did not change, all

52
00:03:15,907 --> 00:03:18,530
that work becomes heat
flowing into the bath.

53
00:03:18,910 --> 00:03:23,699
Both routes, entropy and free
energy, give the same bound.

54
00:03:24,817 --> 00:03:26,030
Let's compute the number.

55
00:03:26,410 --> 00:03:33,811
Boltzmann's constant is
1.380649 × 10⁻²³ J/K.

56
00:03:34,191 --> 00:03:40,570
At room temperature, three hundred
kelvin, and with the natural

57
00:03:40,570 --> 00:03:46,140
log of two, about 0.693, we
get 2.87 × 10⁻²¹ J per bit.

58
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:50,055
The bound grows linearly
with temperature: cooling the

59
00:03:50,055 --> 00:03:53,787
system to four kelvin lowers
it about seventy-five times.

60
00:03:54,167 --> 00:03:55,623
Let's compare with hardware.

61
00:03:56,003 --> 00:04:00,545
According to the syllabus,
one pbit update on a

62
00:04:00,545 --> 00:04:04,507
Z1-type chip costs at most 7.4 × 10⁻¹⁴ J.

63
00:04:04,887 --> 00:04:08,312
That is about twenty-six million
times the Landauer bound.

64
00:04:08,692 --> 00:04:12,441
Today's devices are very far
from the fundamental limit.

65
00:04:13,550 --> 00:04:15,736
Which operations are forced to pay?

66
00:04:16,116 --> 00:04:19,014
The logically irreversible
ones, that is, those

67
00:04:19,014 --> 00:04:21,911
that merge several inputs
into the same output.

68
00:04:22,291 --> 00:04:25,602
The NOT gate is a bijection:
the input can be recovered

69
00:04:25,602 --> 00:04:28,492
from the output, and it
requires no dissipation.

70
00:04:28,872 --> 00:04:33,531
The AND gate, in contrast, sends
three different inputs to output zero.

71
00:04:33,911 --> 00:04:39,432
With uniform inputs, two bits of
information go in and only 0.81 come out.

72
00:04:39,812 --> 00:04:44,814
About 1.19 bits are lost, and
that has a minimum Landauer cost.

73
00:04:45,194 --> 00:04:49,749
Charles Bennett showed the way
out: compute reversibly, copy the

74
00:04:49,749 --> 00:04:53,664
result, then uncompute,
undoing the intermediate steps.

75
00:04:54,044 --> 00:04:57,044
That way, mandatory erasure
is kept to a minimum.

76
00:04:58,150 --> 00:05:01,406
There is a hidden condition:
the Landauer bound is

77
00:05:01,406 --> 00:05:04,206
reached only if erasure
is infinitely slow.

78
00:05:04,586 --> 00:05:08,021
If the protocol lasts a finite
time τ, the system cannot

79
00:05:08,021 --> 00:05:11,333
keep up with equilibrium and
extra work is dissipated.

80
00:05:11,713 --> 00:05:15,554
For slow protocols, that
excess decays as one over τ.

81
00:05:15,934 --> 00:05:19,386
The amber curve shows the
mean work versus protocol

82
00:05:19,386 --> 00:05:23,311
duration: it approaches the
Landauer line, but from above.

83
00:05:23,691 --> 00:05:27,723
This is the first example of a
fundamental trade-off of the course,

84
00:05:27,723 --> 00:05:31,515
which we study in unit three:
computing fast costs more energy.

85
00:05:32,617 --> 00:05:35,531
So why do chips spend
millions of times more?

86
00:05:35,911 --> 00:05:40,274
First, reliability: the probability
that thermal noise flips

87
00:05:40,274 --> 00:05:44,347
a bit decays exponentially
with the barrier height, so a

88
00:05:44,347 --> 00:05:48,057
robust digital bit needs
barriers of tens of k_B T.

89
00:05:48,436 --> 00:05:52,259
Second, communication:
moving a bit along a wire

90
00:05:52,259 --> 00:05:56,242
costs on the order of C
V², unrelated to Landauer.

91
00:05:56,622 --> 00:06:00,926
That is why thermodynamic computing does
not try to reach the Landauer limit,

92
00:06:00,926 --> 00:06:04,615
but to stop paying for suppressing
noise and use it as a resource.

93
00:06:04,995 --> 00:06:07,721
In lab two you will
simulate this erasure and

94
00:06:07,721 --> 00:06:10,871
measure the work as a
function of protocol duration.

95
00:06:11,983 --> 00:06:14,224
This principle appears
throughout the course.

96
00:06:14,604 --> 00:06:19,530
It resolves the Maxwell's demon paradox
from the previous lesson: the demon must

97
00:06:19,530 --> 00:06:23,962
erase its memory, and that erasure
pays back exactly what it had gained.

98
00:06:24,342 --> 00:06:28,204
In unit three we will see work
and heat at the level of a

99
00:06:28,204 --> 00:06:31,659
single trajectory, the
Jarzynski equality and speed

100
00:06:31,659 --> 00:06:35,182
limits, which make the cost
of fast erasure precise.

101
00:06:35,562 --> 00:06:40,288
And in unit five we will compare the
real energy of a pbit with this bound.

102
00:06:41,417 --> 00:06:42,878
Four frequent mistakes.

103
00:06:43,258 --> 00:06:47,843
First, believing every
operation costs k_B T ln 2: only

104
00:06:47,843 --> 00:06:52,011
logically irreversible
erasure has a minimum cost.

105
00:06:52,391 --> 00:06:55,606
Second, thinking the cost is
paid when measuring: in the

106
00:06:55,606 --> 00:06:58,879
Landauer and Bennett formulation
it is paid when erasing.

107
00:06:59,259 --> 00:07:03,298
Third, forgetting that the bound
assumes a completely unknown bit;

108
00:07:03,298 --> 00:07:06,910
if the bit is already known,
there is no entropy to remove.

109
00:07:07,290 --> 00:07:11,419
And fourth, believing Landauer
is what limits chips today;

110
00:07:11,419 --> 00:07:15,051
what dominates are noise
margins and data movement.

111
00:07:16,183 --> 00:07:17,025
Let's summarize.

112
00:07:17,405 --> 00:07:22,240
Erasing a bit reduces the
memory's entropy by k_B ln 2.

113
00:07:22,620 --> 00:07:28,267
The second law forces at
least k_B T ln 2 of heat to be

114
00:07:28,267 --> 00:07:33,503
expelled, about 2.87 ×
10⁻²¹ J at room temperature.

115
00:07:33,883 --> 00:07:36,997
Only logically irreversible
operations pay, and

116
00:07:36,997 --> 00:07:40,044
the bound is reached
only with slow protocols.

117
00:07:40,424 --> 00:07:41,967
Information is physical.

118
00:07:43,083 --> 00:07:45,722
Before moving on, test your understanding.

119
00:07:46,102 --> 00:07:50,164
Compute the minimum heat to erase
one gigabyte at three hundred kelvin.

120
00:07:50,544 --> 00:07:55,504
Compute the cost of resetting a bit that
is already zero with probability 0.9.

121
00:07:55,884 --> 00:08:00,661
Explain why the NOT gate has no
Landauer cost and the AND gate does.

122
00:08:01,041 --> 00:08:03,594
And think about what
happens to the erasure

123
00:08:03,594 --> 00:08:06,146
work if the protocol
runs ten times faster.

124
00:08:06,526 --> 00:08:09,726
The solutions are in this
lesson's exercise sheet.
